<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charlotte, Grand Duchess of Luxembourg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1985">1985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Grand Duchess Charlotte · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charlotte was Grand Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> from 1919 to 1964, succeeding her sister Marie-Adélaïde, who abdicated after being accused of pro-German conduct. A referendum in 1919 confirmed the monarchy under her. During the German occupation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> she led the government in exile from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and broadcast to her people, becoming a symbol of resistance. She abdicated in favour of her son Jean.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlotte-grand-duchess-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="Charlotte, Grand Duchess of Luxembourg" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arnott &amp; Rogers, Montreal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlotte,_Grand_Duchess_of_Luxembourg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charlotte was born on 23 January 1896 at Berg Castle, the second daughter of Grand Duke William IV and Marie Anne of Portugal. Her older sister Marie-Adélaïde had succeeded their father, but her actions during the German occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> were controversial. She was seen as sympathetic to the occupier, there were calls in parliament for her abdication, and she was forced to abdicate in favour of Charlotte on 14 January 1919.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> adopted a new constitution that year. In a referendum on 28 September 1919, 77.8 per cent voted to keep the monarchy with Charlotte as head of state. The constitution severely restricted the monarch's powers, codifying actual practice since the end of the personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1890. On 6 November 1919 she married Prince Felix of Bourbon-Parma; they had six children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>By 1935 she had sold the former ducal palaces of Biebrich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Luxembourg's neutrality was violated on 9 May 1940, when she was at Colmar-Berg. She and her ministers placed themselves under French protection, then went on through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to Portugal after the French capitulation. The Germans proposed to restore her functions, but she refused, mindful of her sister's experience. By 29 August 1940 she was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where she began broadcasting to her homeland on the BBC.</p><p>She travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and met President Roosevelt several times. In 1943 she and the government established themselves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where her more regular broadcasts made her a focus for resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. Her eldest son, Jean, served from 1942 in the Irish Guards. She returned to Luxembourg in April 1945, and in the postwar years received heads of state and raised the country's profile abroad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charlotte abdicated on 12 November 1964 in favour of her son Jean. Her reign is the longest of any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a> monarch since the country became a grand duchy in 1815. She died of cancer at Fischbach Castle on 9 July 1985 and was interred in the Ducal Crypt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>.</p><p>She was the last agnatic member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a> and the last personal recipient of the Golden Rose, an honour that in modern times the Pope has awarded only to churches and shrines. A statue of her stands in the Place Clarefontaine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charlotte, Grand Duchess of Luxembourg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Her conduct towards the German occupiers discredited her, and she abdicated in 1919 in favour of her sister <b>Charlotte</b>, whose reign was confirmed by a referendum that year; the constitutional revision of 1919 also introduced universal suffrage and placed sovereignty in the nation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Berg Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. Died in Fischbach Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.81,6.09&amp;year=1953" aria-label="Berg Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4690 430 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charlotte, Grand Duchess of Luxembourg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5140 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5140 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.81,6.09&amp;year=1953">What was here in 1953? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
